our next speaker has actually spoken at  our virtual conferences this is the  first time we have gotten her in person  and on her way here  she's still new to packing  and so her oil in her  carry-on bag and her toiletry bag  spilled all over all of her clothes  so she had the misfortune of having to  spend the afternoon at fifth avenue  shopping for a whole new wardrobe  so everyone please welcome asuma  hi everyone it's a pleasure to be  amongst all of you in person  thank you for coming thank you to the  new york r conference for having me  apologies in advance  i'm going to be talking about a very  sobering issue  which is substance use disorders in this  country  and how we're approaching it at pursue  care  so first when i refer to substance use  disorders and specifically i'll be  focusing on opioid use disorders we're  referring  to the set  of the very significant social  economic  psychological consequences  due to the use and the misuse of a class  of drugs called opioids  in the past two decades we've lost  nearly  half a million people  to  opioids  and  the trends  show that these numbers have quadrupled  in that time  range to put things into perspective  last year  we've lost nearly 140 people to this  every single day  last year  we're losing people around the clock  every five minutes  by the end of my talk we would have lost  four people  we've seen  an increase in these deaths recently in  the last two years  we've already experienced a very steep  increase in 2020 nearly 30 percent  and another increase on top of that  in 2021 with a 15  increase  these numbers from the cdc  are very concerning  and while kovid19 explains some of this  increase it definitely does not account  for trends that we had already seen  prior to the pandemic  these increases are occurring all over  the country  except for a handful of states  most of the country has seen 50 60 70  increases in opioid overdose deaths  and the reasons for these are complex  and vary from state to state  but we could probably start  understanding why  at the county level among 3 000 or so  counties in america  78 percent do not have an opioid  treatment program  unthinkable  30 percent do not have a provider that  is able to dispense life-saving  medications for people suffering from  opioid use disorders  we see further inequalities and  disparities  looking at  gender  looking at race  we continuously see  over the past few years  that women  black people and hispanic people  have significant barriers of access when  it comes to getting the treatment that  they need namely buprenorphine which is  that life-saving medication that they  need  so this is where we come in  pursue care  i'm the director of analytics and  research  at this company  and our mission is to eliminate  every barrier to access for these  patients  starting off with providing telehealth  capabilities for our patients  telehealth has shown that it reduces  inequalities in access to care  it also comes with significant  advantages such as privacy  for our already heavily stigmatized  population  it also allows to have functionality  that keeps our patients better engaged  and that meets them at their comfort  level  and so with that we're proud to have  telehealth services and in-person  clinics  in connecticut florida kentucky maine  up and down the east coast  and we treat all substance use disorders  mainly opioid use disorder alcohol use  disorder  severe mental illness  and other mental health conditions  a little bit about our app  so through telehealth technology and  also in-person clinics  we deliver life-saving medications  we also deliver digital therapeutics  individual and group therapy  psychiatric care  and medications from our own pharmacy  our goals for the data science team  is detecting trends first and foremost  that involves understanding our patient  population  how they interact with our care  and with that  figuring out what are the outcomes that  are meaningful to us and how can we  monitor them  and with that how can we enact  procedural changes to make sure that we  are constantly optimizing our outcomes  for both our patients and our care team  and so this basically becomes a feedback  loop in and of itself  so first off detecting trends  it really begins with our internal  tooling  when i started at pursue care over a  year ago we were still a startup we are  a startup  and with healthcare we have lots of  vendors which do different things  and have data on our patients and our  care team  and this data because it's distributed  across many platforms is structured  differently  and so the challenge  was to bring all these data sources  together  into our data warehouse  but also augmenting it with publicly  available data  to better understand  patient demographics  socioeconomic indicators whether their  area where they're from is resource  burdened facing housing challenges  transportation challenges  all these factors that help us  understand the challenges that our  patients are facing and help us better  tailor our approach to them  and so my work for the past year has  been marrying all these sources together  in a meaningful way into our data  warehouse  and the other significant portion of my  time was dedicated to building  what's called an internal stack this is  a term that i've borrowed from emily  ryderer  the senior analytics manager at capital  one she has a fantastic blog post about  the importance of investing in an  internal stack and i find this graph  perfect to drive that point home  we have a lot of open source packages at  our disposal in our community to do all  sorts of things from database querying  to  deploying our apps and dashboards  but sometimes those packages are really  built to fulfill as many use cases as  possible in a general sense and you may  be better serviced by building your own  package to not only fulfill specific use  cases that you might have that are not  fulfilled by open source packages or to  save some time  and that's exactly what we've done we've  built packages to automate our  extraction transform loading processes  our database connection and querying api  requests we've built packages that  package together public data from  different sources like the american  census bureau and the cdc  and we've built packages for data  manipulation and data visualizations for  steps that we repeat over and over again  across our reports and dashboards  and that has  led to us  building an internal stack that really  is  helping us in efficiently  detecting trends in an automated  and in a reproducible way which is key  so with this stack we're able to then  look at trends that are meaningful to us  such as patient demographics  socioeconomic indicators  healthcare resource utilization  clinical characteristics  and our patients treatment plan journey  how they're interacting with with their  treatment plan  and the way we deliver this to the  various stakeholders in our company  from physicians  broad care team to the c-suite is  powered by our studio connect our studio  connect is first and foremost a  publishing platform  and really what has paid a lot of  dividends for us is the ability to  schedule  reports and have them pretty much hand  delivered to the hands of our  stakeholders  and that has also led to a bunch of  really positive impacts across the  organization in terms of building data  literacy  building buy-in  and we have a really rich exchange with  the other departments in terms of  what are the outcomes that are  meaningful to us based on this  exploration of the trends  so the next broad goal of ours is once  those outcomes were determined is to  monitor them  the outcomes  the main outcomes that matter to us are  engagement so broadly speaking that  means  how patients interact with our  telehealth platform  are they fulfilling open tasks  are they  logging in to their digital therapeutic  and fulfilling modules there  the other important outcome for us is  retention are we retaining these  patients  unfortunately in this patient population  only 30 to 40 percent of people are  retained in care  and because of that a lot of people get  lost to follow up or end up relapsing or  end up passing away so for us retention  is an extremely important metric by  which we measure the efficacy of our  treatment  drug use over time we  we distribute drug tests regularly to  our patients and it's important to see  their drug use over time whether we're  decreasing it as expected or whether  they're relapsing so we're able in real  time to detect any changes  there  and finally adherence to treatment plan  we've designed treatment plans for our  patients and we want to know whether  they're fulfilling all the milestones  and all  the steps predicated by their treatment  plan  so as you can imagine based on these  outcomes the analysis and modeling of  this type of data requires different  approaches depending on the question and  the outcome  and so we're very lucky to be  well serviced by the tidiverse ecosystem  of packages for both data manipulation  and data wrangling along with some other  ones that are not in that ecosystem but  we make extreme heavy use of such as  lubridate for date time manipulations  janitor and skim r for quick summaries  and tabulations of the data  and for the modeling aspect  we are well serviced once again by the  tidy models ecosystem for all steps  involving modeling really from the  splitting of the data all the way to  deployment  and the brms package  and all of that is  delivered  through our studio connect for our  stakeholders  and so one of the last steps for the  data science team is to inform possible  process changes whether they be  operational so changing the way in which  we deliver the care  but also in ways in which we can  personalize treatments because as we  know for most things one size does not  fit all so with that rich information  that we constantly have access to we can  start  modeling whether there are certain  treatment paths that lead to better  outcome so that is an ongoing work  of ours and we hope to publish on this  soon  and so with that we've completed sort of  the loop of our goals for the data  science team at pursuit care and really  what we're about right now is early  detection of trends  continuous improvement  and hopefully better outcomes for our  care team and our patients  so thank you thank you to you for  attending thank you to  my colleagues thank you to our patients  who continuously  inspire us with their courage and their  tenacity  and thank you to our conference  [Applause]  you
 

